 s8="8. Presets",
 pre_intro="Factory presets are available in the DAW window's preset menu (VST3 programs), via right-click › Factory presets and by MIDI Program Change. Right-click › Save / Load user preset gives 9 user slots shared by every instance (%APPDATA%\\Studio Wiga\\Phase 90\\user_presets.xml on Windows, ~/Library/Application Support/Studio Wiga/Phase 90/ on Mac). Presets store SPEED, the version and the SYNC state. Loading a preset always switches the effect on.",
 pre_hdr=["Preset", "Version", "Hz", "Description"],
 pre=["Knob at noon: today's Phase 90 (M101).", "The smooth, round sound of the first units.", "Slow sweep over an overdriven tone.", "Crunch rhythm, R28 for extra bite.",
      "Very slow “jet plane” effect.", "Clean funk rhythm parts.", "Script top speed, close to vibrato.", "Block top speed, rotary-speaker style.",
      "For bass (Script keeps more low end).", "One sweep per beat, locked to the DAW.", "Two sweeps per beat for funk rhythm parts.", "One slow sweep every two beats."],
 s9="9. Tips",
 tips=["<b>Placement in Reaper</b> — like the other Studio Wiga pedals, put Phase 90 <b>before the amp simulator</b> (Two Notes Genome): the phaser then drives the amp like a real pedal on the floor. After the amp (or after a fuzz), the swoosh becomes more pronounced and airy.",
       "<b>7-string and low tunings</b> — the sweep goes down to ≈ 80 Hz, so the low G (≈ 49 Hz) and lower strings are phased too. For heavy riffs, Script keeps a firmer low end; Block adds bite in the mids. Slow speeds (Slow Jet, Eruption) work best on low, sustained chords.",
       "<b>Rhythm vs lead</b> — Funk Rhythm / Classic Block for clean rhythm, Eruption / Brown Sound for overdriven leads. Learn SPEED on an expression pedal (default CC 81) to accelerate the sweep like a rotary speaker.",
       "<b>In time with the song</b> — SYNC 1/4 on a riff, 1/8 for funk rhythm, 1/2 for slow swells on long chords. Start playback in Reaper and the sweep lands on the beat at every bar; learn the SYNC button on a footswitch (default CC 83) to change division live.",
       "<b>Mix</b> — like the original there is no depth or mix knob: the 50/50 blend is what creates the deep notches. In a stereo mix, try Stereo spread on a doubled guitar."],
